# Font Library System

## Product and Technical Concept Spec

## 1. Core Concept

The Font Library System is a local asset management and preview system for organizing, searching, tagging, licensing, comparing, and selecting fonts for creative projects.

The purpose is to turn a large folder of fonts into a usable typography asset library.

The system should help answer:

What fonts do I have?
What licenses do they have?
What do they look like?
Which fonts fit a specific vibe or project?
Which fonts pair well together?
Which fonts are safe to use in public or commercial assets?
Which fonts have the glyph coverage I need?
Which fonts have already been used in my projects?

This system is the first practical step before building a full font generator.

## 2. Business Purpose

The user already has access to approximately 1,800 fonts. The immediate business need is not necessarily to generate more fonts. The immediate need is to make the existing font library usable.

A large font collection only becomes valuable when it can be searched, previewed, filtered, and reused consistently.

The Font Library System supports:

Brand development
Music release artwork
Lyric videos
Music videos
Prophetic Preprint visuals
AI Psalm visual systems
Asabaal’s Academic Adventures
Social media templates
Title cards
Presentation graphics
Meaning systems
Client facing design assets

The system should reduce design friction by making font choice faster, more intentional, and more legally trackable.

## 3. Primary Deliverable

The first deliverable is a local font inventory and preview system.

It should produce:

A searchable font manifest
License tracking
Generated preview images
Font specimen sheets
Project based collections
Vibe tags
Font comparison views
Glyph coverage reports
Recommended font shortlists

## 4. System Inputs

The system should accept:

A local folder of font files
A Google Fonts repository folder
License files
Optional project names
Optional user tags
Optional sample text presets
Optional reference images or mood categories later

Supported font formats:

`.ttf`
`.otf`
`.woff` if useful later
`.woff2` if useful later

The first version should prioritize `.ttf` and `.otf`.

## 5. Core Data Model

Each font record should track:

Font family
Font style
Font weight
Font width if available
Italic or upright status
File path
File format
Source folder
License type
License file path
Copyright string
Designer or foundry metadata if available
Glyph count
Supported characters
Supported scripts if detectable
Preview image paths
Specimen sheet path
User tags
Project tags
Usage history
Favorite status
Archived status
Notes

## 6. License Tracking

License tracking is a core feature, not an afterthought.

For each font, the system should attempt to identify:

License type
License file
Whether commercial use appears allowed
Whether modification appears allowed
Whether redistribution appears allowed
Attribution requirements if present
Source location

The system does not need to provide legal advice, but it should preserve license evidence and make the source license visible.

Recommended license status labels:

Clear
Needs review
Unknown
Do not use
Commercial safe
Internal only

Google Fonts under the `ofl` directory should be easier to classify because each family usually includes license metadata.

## 7. Preview Generation

The system should render preview images for each font.

Preview types:

## 7.1 Quick Preview

Shows the font name rendered in its own font.

Example text:

Asabaal
Prophetic Preprint
The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog
ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ
abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
0123456789

## 7.2 Alphabet Sheet

Shows:

Uppercase letters
Lowercase letters
Numbers
Basic punctuation

## 7.3 Brand Phrase Sheet

Uses project relevant phrases:

ASABAAL
ASABAAL VENTURES
PROPHETIC PREPRINT
AS GOD HAS SAID
I NEVER ASKED TO BE QUEER
ACADEMIC ADVENTURES
RECONCILIATION
MEANING SYSTEMS

## 7.4 Use Case Preview

Renders the font in actual design contexts:

Title card
Subtitle
Caption
Album title
Scripture quote
Lyric line
Academic heading
Logo wordmark

