## 9. Search and Filter

The system should support searching and filtering by:

Font family
Style
Weight
Tag
License status
Project use
Glyph coverage
Favorite status
Recently added
Recently used
Use case
File format
Source directory

Example searches:

Sacred serif with commercial safe license
Academic body text fonts
Sharp display fonts for title cards
Soft sans fonts for subtitles
Fonts used in Prophetic Preprint
Fonts with full uppercase and number coverage
Fonts safe for album art

## 10. Font Comparison

The system should support comparing fonts side by side.

Comparison views:

Same phrase across multiple fonts
Alphabet sheets across multiple fonts
Title card preview across multiple fonts
Font A versus Font B
Serif candidates
Subtitle candidates
Logo candidates

Comparison should help answer:

Which one feels more sacred?
Which one is more readable?
Which one fits the project better?
Which one pairs better with the heading font?
Which one works at small sizes?
Which one has better numbers?

## 11. Font Pairing

The system should eventually support font pairing.

A pairing record might include:

Heading font
Body font
Caption font
Subtitle font
Logo font
Use case
Project
Notes

Example pairings:

Prophetic Preprint title font plus academic body font
AI Psalm display font plus readable subtitle font
Academic Adventures playful heading plus clean body font
Album art title font plus lyric caption font

Font pairing can start manually and later become AI assisted.

## 12. Project Collections

The system should support collections.

A collection is a curated group of fonts for a project or use case.

Examples:

Prophetic Preprint Candidates
AI Psalm Title Fonts
Academic Adventures Fonts
Asabaal Brand Serif Candidates
Lyric Video Subtitle Fonts
Sacred Editorial Fonts
Warm Humanist Sans Fonts
High Contrast Display Fonts

Collections should allow notes and rankings.

## 13. Usage Tracking

The system should track where fonts are used.

For each font, store:

Project used in
Asset type
Date used
Output file if known
Notes
Whether it worked well
Whether it should be reused

This prevents losing track of typography decisions across projects.

## 14. Favorite and Archive System

The system should distinguish between:

Favorite fonts
Candidate fonts
Rejected fonts
Archived fonts
Unknown fonts

Not all 1,800 fonts will be useful.

The goal is not to treat every font equally. The goal is to progressively identify the fonts that matter.

Useful workflow:

Import all fonts
Generate previews
Tag obvious categories
Favorite strong candidates
Archive weak or irrelevant fonts
Build shortlists for actual projects

## 15. AI Assistance Layer

The first version does not require AI, but AI can help.

AI can assist with:

Suggesting vibe tags from preview sheets
Ranking fonts for a project brief
Comparing two fonts
Recommending pairings
Identifying similar fonts
Summarizing font personality
Detecting which fonts look ceremonial, academic, playful, etc.
Generating collection names
Suggesting which fonts should be used for headings, body text, or captions

AI should not replace the manifest and preview system. It should sit on top of it.

## 16. User Interface Concept

The system could start as a CLI and static HTML gallery before becoming a full app.

## 16.1 CLI First

Useful commands:

Scan font folder
Generate manifest
Generate previews
Generate specimen sheets
Search fonts
Add tag
Add to collection
Mark favorite
Mark archived
Export collection

Example conceptual commands:

scan fonts
render previews
list sacred serif candidates
compare fonts
create project collection
export Prophetic Preprint shortlist

## 16.2 Static HTML Gallery

A static HTML gallery could show:

Font cards
Preview images
License status
Tags
Favorite button later
Collection grouping
Searchable index

This is probably the fastest useful visual interface.

## 16.3 Future Web App

Later, the system could become a proper local web app with:

Search
Filters
Comparison mode
Tag editing
Collection management
AI recommendations
Project usage tracking
Preview customization

